{"kind":"request","text":"Design a state seed: a read that serves a scope's state at a head, and a way to load that state into `MemoryState`. A design note comes first. No code in this request before the note is approved.\n\nFiled 2026-11-12 22:26 EST at Hugh's request, from the dap-foldkit plan (`~/play/dap-foldkit/PLAN.md`, draft 3 revision 3, changes D3 and D6). Facts marked \"read\" are read at main `7ea170c9`. Facts marked \"M0\" come from `~/play/dap-foldkit/probe/RESULTS.md`, a probe that another session ran on 2026-11-12 against main `a76764a7`. I did not rerun it.\n\nWhy this matters. A browser replica folds the scope's journal from entry 0 on each load, with `applyEntry`. That has three limits.\n- Time. The fold is close to linear at about 55 ms for each 1,000 entries (M0, Node and headless Chrome). A load stays under 1 s to about 15,000 to 20,000 entries. Memory is not measured. A journal holds up to 100,000 entries (`scopeEntries`, `packages/contract/src/bounds.ts:120`, read).\n- A floor. Below a floor the `log` read answers `archived` (`packages/scope/src/reads.ts:301`, read). A replica cannot start from above a floor, so an archived room cannot have a replica at all. The archive is dap's own remedy for a full journal, so the two features exclude each other today.\n- A copy. A client that predicts a second act over the predicted state of a first act needs a second state. `MemoryState` has no copy and no load.\n\nWhat exists (read).\n- `StateSnapshot` and `stateDigest(snapshot)` (`packages/derive/src/state.ts:174` and `:219`). `MemoryState.all()` returns a snapshot (`state.ts:644`), and `snapshot()` returns its canonical text (`state.ts:670`).\n- A `checkpoint` entry carries `{ through, state: Digest }` (`packages/contract/src/entry.ts:79`), and `packages/derive/src/settle.ts:295` and `:307` write and check that digest. A served snapshot can therefore be checked against an entry in the hash chain.\n- No read serves a snapshot. `MemoryState` has no constructor or method that takes one.\n\nWhat the note owes.\n1. Loading: a function that builds a `MemoryState` from a `StateSnapshot`, with the proof that fold(load(snapshot), later entries) equals fold(all entries). The same function gives the copy.\n2. The read: which head it serves (any head, or a checkpoint's `through` only), how a member's session is admitted, the size bound of the answer, and how the page continues.\n3. Trust: how a client checks the served state. A checkpoint digest in the chain is one way. The note states what a client that holds no entry below the floor can and cannot verify.\n4. Placement: whether the read is kernel or a service, and which existing read names and record forms it uses. The coherence filing rule applies: the note names the kernel item it closes, or shows that it adds no record form, no entry kind and no field type.\n5. Limits: what a snapshot does not carry. Entries that change no item, such as acts of step `comment`, are in the journal and not in the state.\n\nSize: medium. The note is small. The load function is small. The read is the larger part.\n\nNeeded by: no current dap-foldkit milestone. M2 to M4 fold from entry 0, and presence (M5) uses no replica. The first user is a recorded room past about 20,000 entries, or any room with a floor. Place it after `ScopeHandle.log` (the D1 request filed with this one).","body":{"conditions":"A design note lands in notes/ that states: how a StateSnapshot loads into MemoryState and why a fold from the loaded state equals a fold from entry 0; the read that serves a snapshot (which heads, admission, size bound, paging); how a client checks the served state against the hash chain and what it cannot check below a floor; kernel or service placement under the coherence filing rule; and what a snapshot does not carry. The note carries a status line and names the heads that follow. No code lands under this request.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}